---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';

const title = "Zoology Career Options in India: 7 Routes, Field Pay and Business Layers";
const seoTitle = "Zoology Career Options: 7 Routes, Real Field Pay, What Scales";
const description =
  "Zoology career options in India: seven routes from fisheries and bees to wildlife and NET, with sourced field pay (Rs 25,000 a month) and what can scale.";
const canonical = "https://futurecareerschool.com/blog/career-options/zoology-career-options/";
const publishedDateISO = "2026-10-14";
const publishedDateLabel = "14 October 2026";
const authorName = "Allu Vagdevi";
const authorUrl = "https://futurecareerschool.com/about/allu-vagdevi/";
const authorRole = "Career researcher and writer";

const jumpLinks = [
  { id: "stages", label: "What each degree stage opens" },
  { id: "routes", label: "Seven routes, ordered by growth room" },
  { id: "field-pay", label: "Field pay and the cost of getting in" },
  { id: "funnels", label: "The second filter in wildlife and research" },
  { id: "who-pays", label: "Who pays, and how they trust you" },
  { id: "dissection", label: "What changed in zoology practicals" },
  { id: "ai", label: "What AI changes in zoology work" },
  { id: "checkpoint", label: "The 4-Checkpoint Protocol" },
  { id: "gates", label: "The 3 Gates" },
  { id: "mistakes", label: "Expensive wrong turns" },
  { id: "first-test", label: "Your first short test" },
  { id: "faq", label: "FAQs" },
];

const stageRows = [
  {
    stage: "After BSc Zoology",
    opens: "Lab and project assistant posts, B.Ed and school teaching, state and central exams that name the subject, MSc entry. A 2024 Central Silk Board institute project notice, as reported, listed Zoology among accepted BSc subjects, but it was a contract post tied to one project.",
    add: "One applied skill: fish or bee farming basics, GIS, statistics or a lab method. Plus one field or lab log you can show.",
    rate: "Not so scalable on the degree alone. Scalable with a business layer when you add a farm, lab or teaching business.",
  },
  {
    stage: "After MSc Zoology",
    opens: "Project fellow and research biologist posts at conservation bodies, ZSI and university projects, PGT teaching, NET and JRF entry.",
    add: "Field survey method, data analysis in R or Python, report writing, and one species or habitat you know deeply.",
    rate: "Not so scalable as a salaried researcher. Scalable with a business layer through consulting, testing or training.",
  },
  {
    stage: "After JRF or PhD",
    opens: "Scientist, faculty and senior research posts, if a post is open. A fellowship is training, not a job.",
    add: "Papers, a funded project, supervision record and a skill that works outside academia.",
    rate: "Not so scalable. Several filters stack up, so keep an industry or consulting skill running.",
  },
];

const routes = [
  {
    badge: "Route 1",
    title: "Bioinformatics, data and science communication",
    tone: "teal",
    scale: "high",
    scaleLabel: "Scalable",
    entry: "BSc or MSc Zoology plus statistics, basic Python or R, and GIS. Writers need clear English and a few finished pieces.",
    take: "Here the zoology degree stops being the whole product. Species data, camera-trap images and field surveys all need someone who can clean, analyse and explain them, and the skill can be sold remotely to many employers. Reliable pay data for zoology plus data is thin, so treat it as a skill stack, not a job title.",
    layer: "Freelance analysis for NGOs and researchers, then a small consulting service. Writers can add a newsletter or channel.",
    best: "Students who like animals and screens in equal parts.",
    watch: "Paying for a costly bioinformatics course before you have finished one small analysis of a public dataset.",
  },
  {
    badge: "Route 2",
    title: "Fisheries and aquaculture",
    tone: "gold",
    scale: "mid",
    scaleLabel: "Scalable with a business layer",
    entry: "Zoology MSc with fish biology, hatchery and water-quality work. The ICAR fisheries master's route (CIFE) is reported to ask for a BFSc, BSc Fisheries, BVSc or BSc Agriculture degree, so a plain BSc Zoology may not qualify. Check the current ICAR notice.",
    take: "India is the second-largest fish producer, about 8 percent of world output. Production rose from 96 lakh tonnes in 2013-14 to 195 lakh tonnes in 2024-25, and the sector supports over 30 million livelihoods (PIB, 20 November 2025). Many fisheries posts and the ICAR master's route are written around fisheries degrees, so check whether a zoology MSc qualifies before you plan on a salaried post.",
    layer: "A hatchery, fish-health and water-testing service, a feed or seed supply business, or an advisory service for farmers. Licences and local rules differ by state, so verify them.",
    best: "Students comfortable with ponds, farmers and early mornings.",
    watch: "Assuming BSc Zoology and BFSc are the same door. They are not.",
  },
  {
    badge: "Route 3",
    title: "Bees, silk and poultry: the farm-animal business layers",
    tone: "violet",
    scale: "mid",
    scaleLabel: "Scalable with a business layer",
    entry: "BSc or MSc Zoology plus short training from the relevant board or institute. Sericulture has its own diploma route at Central Silk Board institutes.",
    take: "India made about 1.4 lakh tonnes of honey in 2024 and exported about 1.07 lakh tonnes in FY 2023-24 (PIB), under a Rs 500 crore beekeeping mission. Silk supports tens of lakhs of people, though official counts differ by year (80.90 lakh projected up to January 2025, 94.80 lakh for 2023-24). Poultry gave 48.96 percent of meat output in 2023-24, with about 142.77 billion eggs (secondary summary of animal husbandry statistics).",
    layer: "An apiary with your own honey brand and lab-tested batches, silkworm rearing or seed supply, or a poultry health and feed advisory. These are real businesses, with capital, disease risk and price swings.",
    best: "Students who can handle risk, selling and daily animal care.",
    watch: "Starting a unit before you have worked on one. Spend time on a working farm first.",
  },
  {
    badge: "Route 4",
    title: "Lab, medical and pharma support",
    tone: "teal",
    scale: "mid",
    scaleLabel: "Scalable with a business layer",
    entry: "MSc Zoology with parasitology, entomology, toxicology or cell biology, then diagnostic, quality, clinical research or animal-testing lab roles.",
    take: "Roles exist in diagnostic labs, pharma quality teams and research units, but we found no sourced pay figures for zoology graduates in them, so we print none. Career blogs list ranges without a source or date. Read live postings for your city before you decide.",
    layer: "A testing or diagnostic collection service once you hold the licences and a referral base. It is a later stage, not a first job.",
    best: "Students who like method, detail and documentation.",
    watch: "A role that quietly asks for a pharmacy, microbiology or MLT degree you do not have. Read the posting line by line.",
  },
  {
    badge: "Route 5",
    title: "Teaching",
    tone: "gold",
    scale: "mid",
    scaleLabel: "Scalable with a business layer",
    entry: "B.Ed after BSc for school levels, MSc plus B.Ed for senior classes, and NET or the state test for colleges. Kendriya Vidyalaya notices ask for Botany, Zoology and Chemistry at graduation level for TGT Science and CTET Paper II.",
    take: "A salaried school or college post follows a fixed pay scale and has few seats per notice. The business layer is teaching beyond the school: NEET and CUET biology coaching, a paid online course, or a YouTube channel with a clear audience. Teaching quality and proof count more than the platform.",
    layer: "Small-batch tuition, a recorded course and a community, priced by outcome.",
    best: "Students who explain well and enjoy repeating ideas until they land.",
    watch: "Counting on a government teaching post alone. Notices are few and each has its own subject list.",
  },
  {
    badge: "Route 6",
    title: "Research: NET, JRF, ZSI and ICAR",
    tone: "violet",
    scale: "low",
    scaleLabel: "Not so scalable",
    entry: "MSc, then CSIR-UGC NET (Life Sciences paper for most zoology graduates), a university test, or a project post at ZSI.",
    take: "The CSIR JRF pays Rs 37,000 a month for two years and Rs 42,000 as SRF, with a Rs 20,000 yearly contingency grant, for a master's holder at 55 percent (50 percent reserved) and age up to 30 with relaxations. It pays like an entry job and buys training, not a post. A February 2026 ZSI notice reportedly listed 20 JRF posts at Rs 37,000 plus HRA for one year at first.",
    layer: "Keep a data, GIS or science-writing skill that sells outside the lab, and plan consulting once you have a niche.",
    best: "Students who enjoy long, uncertain projects.",
    watch: "An MSc taken only to delay a decision. A PhD needs a funded seat, a supervisor and an exit plan.",
  },
  {
    badge: "Route 7",
    title: "Wildlife biology and forest service",
    tone: "gold",
    scale: "low",
    scaleLabel: "Not so scalable",
    entry: "MSc Wildlife Science at WII, or an MSc in Zoology, Ecology or Wildlife Science for project posts, or a degree naming Zoology for the Indian Forest Service.",
    take: "This is the dream most students arrive with and the narrowest door. WII lists 20 seats (15 Indian), and the Indian Forest Service had about 80 vacancies in 2026 against 1,046 candidates in mains. Conservation project posts are contracts, often for a year at a time.",
    layer: "Nature-guide and tour services, camera-trap or survey consulting for companies that need ecological reports, and science content. Each needs credibility and clients.",
    best: "Students who want field life more than money, and have already spent time in a forest or reserve.",
    watch: "A wildlife dream with no field exposure. Volunteer once before you commit years and fees.",
  },
];

const payRows = [
  { route: "WII MSc Wildlife Science", cost: "About Rs 6,00,000 fee for Indian students; 15 Indian seats; full scholarships for 8 Indian students on merit", pay: "Stipend Rs 3,000 a month, subject to funds", sum: "3,000 x 24 months = Rs 72,000, which is 12 percent of the fee. A scholarship seat changes the sum completely, so ask for last year's scholarship list." },
  { route: "BNHS Junior Project Fellow (notice 34/2025)", cost: "MSc in Wildlife Science, Ecology, Environmental Science, Zoology or related; age up to 30", pay: "Rs 25,000 a month, one-year contract, extendable", sum: "25,000 x 12 = Rs 3,00,000 a year. The WII fee equals 2.0 years of this pay." },
  { route: "BNHS Project Fellow, Indian Skimmer (notice 09/2026)", cost: "MSc in Life Sciences, Zoology, Environmental or Wildlife Science; Hindi needed; extended summer fieldwork", pay: "Rs 25,000 a month, one-year professional contract", sum: "Same Rs 3,00,000 a year, with long stays away from home." },
  { route: "BNHS Research Biologist, conservation breeding", cost: "Higher qualification and experience (read from a search listing, not opened in full)", pay: "Rs 55,000 a month, renewable contract", sum: "55,000 x 12 = Rs 6,60,000. A senior step, still a contract." },
  { route: "CSIR JRF, then SRF", cost: "MSc at 55 percent plus NET; age up to 30", pay: "Rs 37,000 a month for two years, then Rs 42,000", sum: "37,000 x 12 = Rs 4,44,000; 42,000 x 12 = Rs 5,04,000. A BNHS project fellow earns 68 percent of the JRF figure." },
  { route: "Career-blog ranges for a zoologist", cost: "Not stated", pay: "Rs 3 to 8 lakh a year, with no source or date", sum: "We did not use these. Two of the blogs we read repeat roles and print different ranges for the same job." },
];

const funnelRows = [
  { ladder: "Wildlife scientist", first: "WII MSc Wildlife Science: 20 seats, 15 for Indians, entrance test plus interview in Dehradun (50 percent test, 50 percent interview)", second: "Project posts that run one year at a time, then a few permanent posts in forest, research or institute roles", note: "Each filter is separate. A seat at WII does not carry a job with it." },
  { ladder: "Forest officer", first: "A degree that names Zoology or another listed subject", second: "IFoS 2026: about 80 vacancies, 1,046 qualified prelims for mains", note: "1,046 / 80 = 13.1 mains candidates per vacancy, after a first filter that already removed most applicants." },
  { ladder: "Researcher", first: "MSc, then NET or a university test for a funded place", second: "A PhD seat, then a scientist or faculty post", note: "Each stage needs a supervisor, funding and a gap in the right lab." },
];

const whoPaysRows = [
  { route: "Data and science writing", pays: "NGOs, researchers, companies, publishers", trust: "A public analysis or a few finished articles", skill: "A simple offer: one report type at a clear price, with a sample" },
  { route: "Fisheries and aquaculture", pays: "Fish farmers, hatcheries, feed and seed firms", trust: "Referrals from other farmers, water-test results, a working demo pond", skill: "Proof: a before and after record from one farm" },
  { route: "Bees, silk, poultry", pays: "Households buying a brand, traders, processors, schemes", trust: "Lab-tested batches, a traceable source, repeat buyers", skill: "Follow-up and referrals; watch one number, repeat orders" },
  { route: "Lab and pharma support", pays: "Employers; later patients and clinics for a lab you own", trust: "Interview, certificates, licences, referrals from doctors", skill: "A clear explanation of the method you know" },
  { route: "Teaching", pays: "School or college; students and parents for tuition", trust: "Results, demo classes, word of mouth", skill: "Positioning: one exam and one level you teach well" },
  { route: "Research", pays: "Funding agencies and institutes", trust: "Papers, supervisor, project record", skill: "Employer-only for now. The sales layer matters for the consulting route after it" },
  { route: "Wildlife and forest", pays: "Forest departments and conservation bodies; companies for ecological reports", trust: "Field record, species lists, references from project leads", skill: "Proof first: a survey report you finished and can share. Government posts are employer-only" },
];

const weekCards = [
  { who: "Field biologist on a project", body: "Early starts, transects, nest or camera checks, heat or rain, local helpers, then data entry at night. Long absences from home are normal." },
  { who: "Hatchery or farm technician", body: "Water tests, feed schedules, disease checks, records and farmer calls. Weekends are not free during breeding season." },
  { who: "Lab technician", body: "Sample handling, protocol, documentation and instrument care. Repetitive and exact, with little animal contact." },
  { who: "Teacher", body: "Lesson plans, marking, doubt-clearing and parent meetings. Practical classes are now partly field-based or digital." },
  { who: "Data and GIS analyst", body: "Cleaning survey tables, mapping habitats, making charts, and explaining them to people who do not know the species." },
];

const checkpointItems = [
  { num: "01", title: "Biology: where do you work best?", body: "Remote forest camp, pond, lab bench, classroom or laptop? Answer from your last busy week, not your ideal one.", note: "Field routes test stamina and family patience. Lab and data routes test focus." },
  { num: "02", title: "Context: what can your family carry?", body: "Rs 25,000 a month on a one-year contract, a Rs 6 lakh fee, or capital for a farm. Name the number your family can carry.", note: "Write the fee next to the pay in the ledger above." },
  { num: "03", title: "Market: who actually pays?", body: "Use the who-pays table. If you cannot name a payer for your route, you do not yet have a route.", note: "A seat count is not a demand count." },
  { num: "04", title: "Survival: what if this gate closes?", body: "Name the second route and the skill that carries over. Name the task in your route that AI tools will shrink.", note: "A good plan survives a missed seat or a lapsed contract." },
];

const gateItems = [
  { num: "Gate 1", title: "Proof of Skill", body: "One finished piece: a species checklist from a local site with dates and photos, a water-quality log from a pond, or a cleaned dataset with three charts." },
  { num: "Gate 2", title: "Proof of Communication", body: "Explain that work in thirty seconds to two minutes to someone who is not a biologist, in your language and in English." },
  { num: "Gate 3", title: "Proof of Value", body: "Three people who work in the route, such as a project biologist, a fish farmer or a teacher, say the work is useful and name what is missing." },
];

const mistakes = [
  { num: "01", title: "Picking the degree name and skipping the exam notice", body: "Forest, teaching and research notices list the subjects they accept. Read the notice before you choose the BSc subject." },
  { num: "02", title: "Paying a large fee for a small-pool programme", body: "A Rs 6 lakh programme against Rs 25,000 a month project pay needs a scholarship or a named path to a paid role. Ask for the numbers." },
  { num: "03", title: "Treating a fellowship as a job", body: "A JRF is Rs 4,44,000 a year of training. It is not a post, and a contract that runs one year at a time is not security." },
  { num: "04", title: "Starting a farm unit before working on one", body: "Disease, water and price swings catch beginners. Work on a live unit first and keep your savings out of the first trial." },
  { num: "05", title: "A degree with no field or lab record", body: "Employers and NGOs read finished work. A species list, a lab log or a short report beats a stack of certificates." },
];

const testSteps = [
  { title: "Pick two routes, not seven", body: "Run both through the 4-Checkpoint Protocol and set the rest aside." },
  { title: "Open the real notice for each", body: "Read the qualification, age and pay lines on the official page or advertisement, not a blog summary." },
  { title: "Do one small piece of work in each", body: "A local species survey, a pond visit with a farmer, a recorded explainer, or a small analysis of a public dataset. Go at your own pace." },
  { title: "Ask three people who do the work", body: "One from each route and one who left it. Ask what the first year felt like and what they earned." },
  { title: "Drop one route in writing", body: "If you cannot drop one, you are still comparing labels." },
];

const faqItems = [
  { q: "What are the career options in zoology?", a: "Seven routes are worth comparing: bioinformatics, data and science communication; fisheries and aquaculture; bees, silk and poultry businesses; lab and pharma support; teaching; research through NET and JRF; and wildlife biology or forest service. Pick by who pays, the exam or notice you must clear, and the skill you add to the degree." },
  { q: "Is BSc Zoology a good career?", a: "It is a good base for students who add a second skill and a record of work. On its own it leads mostly to contract, lab-assistant and teaching-track roles. Pay and growth come from the route you add: data, fisheries, bees, teaching or research." },
  { q: "What can I do after BSc Zoology?", a: "You can do an MSc, a B.Ed for school teaching, an entry-level lab or project post, or start building a skill such as GIS, statistics or aquaculture basics. Some exams name Zoology as an accepted subject, so read the notice. Most senior roles in wildlife and research ask for an MSc." },
  { q: "Can a zoology graduate become an IFS officer?", a: "Zoology is among the subjects named in the Indian Forest Service notice as we read it in our biology options guide. The exam is long: about 80 vacancies in 2026 and 1,046 candidates in mains. Keep a second income path while you prepare." },
  { q: "How much does a zoologist earn in India?", a: "There is no reliable national figure. Sourced field data: BNHS project fellow posts at Rs 25,000 a month on one-year contracts, and the CSIR JRF at Rs 37,000 a month. Career blogs print Rs 3 to 8 lakh a year without a source. Check live postings and ask people in the route." },
  { q: "Which NET paper should a zoology student take?", a: "Zoology graduates normally sit the Life Sciences paper of CSIR-UGC NET, but confirm on the current NTA bulletin. For JRF you need a master's at 55 percent (50 percent for reserved categories) and age up to 30 with relaxations. A four-year bachelor's at 75 percent gives JRF and PhD entry, not assistant professor." },
  { q: "Can I work in wildlife without WII?", a: "Yes. Conservation bodies such as BNHS post project roles for MSc holders in Zoology, Ecology, Environmental or Wildlife Science. Pay is contract-based and fieldwork is heavy. A finished survey report and field experience matter more than the institute name." },
  { q: "Should I do an MSc in Zoology?", a: "Do it when your target route asks for it: research, PGT teaching, ZSI or conservation project posts. Keep to the 10 percent education budget heuristic and treat anything above 20 percent as a caution that needs course-specific proof. Avoid an education loan unless the credential is required for the job." },
  { q: "Do zoology students still dissect animals?", a: "UGC guidelines posted in November 2011 said BSc students should not use animals in experiments, faculty may dissect one species for demonstration, and MSc students may dissect two. Field visits and digital tools fill the gap, so build skills through field logs and data work." },
  { q: "Can zoology lead to my own business?", a: "Yes, through fisheries, beekeeping, sericulture or poultry services, a testing or diagnostic lab, a tuition or course business, or a data and consulting service. Each needs licences, capital or a client base, so treat ownership as a later stage after you have worked in the field." },
  { q: "Will AI replace zoologists?", a: "AI is taking over image sorting from camera traps and some report drafting. It does not replace fieldwork, animal handling, farmer trust or judgment about what the data means. The risk is highest for junior tasks that are pure data entry." },
];

const sources = [
  { label: "Wildlife Institute of India: MSc Wildlife Science (seats, fees, scholarships, eligibility)", href: "https://wii.gov.in/msc-in-wildlife-science" },
  { label: "CSIR-HRDG: revised JRF guidelines (stipend, eligibility, age)", href: "https://csirhrdg.res.in/Home/Index/1/Default/3384/60" },
  { label: "PIB: World Fisheries Day 2025, India's fish production and livelihoods", href: "https://static.pib.gov.in/WriteReadData/specificdocs/documents/2025/nov/doc20251120700601.pdf" },
  { label: "PIB: National Beekeeping and Honey Mission", href: "https://static.pib.gov.in/WriteReadData/specificdocs/documents/2025/nov/doc2025112682601.pdf" },
  { label: "PIB: Parliament question on raw silk production in 2024-25", href: "https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=2117111&reg=48&lang=2" },
  { label: "BNHS: Junior Project Fellow, Lesser Florican (notice 34/2025)", href: "https://www.bnhs.org/public/career_pdf/1765183495.pdf" },
  { label: "BNHS: Project Fellow, Indian Skimmer (notice 09/2026)", href: "https://www.bnhs.org/public/career_pdf/PF_01_Uganga_Feb2026.pdf" },
  { label: "Free Press Journal: IFoS 2026 prelims result, 1,046 qualified", href: "https://www.freepressjournal.in/education/upsc-ifs-result-2026-out-at-upscgovin-1046-candidates-clear-prelims-for-forest-service-mains" },
  { label: "Down To Earth: UGC guidelines on phasing out dissection (December 2011)", href: "https://www.downtoearth.org.in/amp/story/environment/ugc-guidelines-on-phasing-out-animal-dissection-spark-controversy-34574" },
];

const faqJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: item.a },
  })),
};
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  seoTitle={seoTitle}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="career-options"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Zoology career options in India split into seven routes, and only a few of them grow past a fixed pay band. The ones that scale are the ones that add a business or a data skill to the degree; the dream route, wildlife, is the narrowest and pays like a stipend at the start.</strong>
    Choosing by who pays and by the skill you add is how a zoology degree becomes a high-value skill portfolio that unlocks better income options and earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Wildlife project posts at BNHS list Rs 25,000 a month on one-year contracts. The WII course fee of about Rs 6,00,000 equals 2.0 years of that pay, unless you win one of the 8 merit scholarships.</li>
      <li>The routes with the most room are data and science communication, fisheries and aquaculture, and bee, silk and poultry businesses. Wildlife and research are Not so scalable and need a second skill beside them.</li>
      <li><strong>Your next step: pick two routes, read the real notice for each, and finish one small piece of work in each before you spend on an MSc.</strong></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    Students ask the same questions again and again: "what can I do after BSc Zoology?", "is zoology a good career?" and "can I become a wildlife officer?". Most pages answer with a list of eighteen job titles and unsourced pay. Here each route has a payer, an entry rule from the body that sets it, and a scalability label.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the subject choice is still open, our <a href="/blog/career-options/biology-career-options-after-12th/">biology career options after 12th</a> guide shows which BSc subject opens which exam. For research tracks after the MSc, see <a href="/blog/career-options/life-sciences-and-biological-sciences-career-options/">life sciences career options</a>. All guides sit in <a href="/blog/career-options/">career options</a>, and <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/career-counselling-for-pcb-students/">career counselling for PCB students</a> helps you test your shortlist against your marks, budget and family.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="stages">What each degree stage opens</h2>

  <p>
    A zoology degree is a ladder with different jobs on each rung. The BSc opens few doors by itself. The MSc opens the field and research posts. Rating follows our rule: Scalable means income and reach can grow through skills, consulting or a business of your own.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Stage</th>
          <th>What it opens</th>
          <th>What to add</th>
          <th>How we rate it</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {stageRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Stage"><strong>{row.stage}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="What it opens">{row.opens}</td>
            <td data-label="What to add">{row.add}</td>
            <td data-label="How we rate it">{row.rate}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="routes">Seven routes, ordered by growth room</h2>

  <p>
    The order is a judgement about how far income and independence can stretch with skill and proof, not about prestige. Data and science communication lead because the skill sells to many employers. Fisheries, bees and silk come next because the market is large and a business layer exists. Teaching and lab roles sit in the middle. Research and wildlife come last because each has a hard entry stage and a separate hard advancement stage.
  </p>

  <div class="lane-grid">
    {routes.map((r) => (
      <div class:list={["lane-card", `lane-card--${r.tone}`]}>
        <span class="lane-card__badge">{r.badge}</span>
        <span class:list={["scale-pill", `scale-pill--${r.scale}`]}>{r.scaleLabel}</span>
        <div class="lane-card__title">{r.title}</div>
        <p class="lane-card__entry"><strong>How you enter:</strong> {r.entry}</p>
        <p>{r.take}</p>
        <div class="lane-row lane-row--layer"><span>Business layer</span> {r.layer}</div>
        <div class="lane-row lane-row--fit"><span>Best for</span> {r.best}</div>
        <div class="lane-row lane-row--watch"><span>Watch out</span> {r.watch}</div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>Scalable ownership inside zoology:</strong> a hatchery or fish-health service, an apiary with your own tested honey brand, a silkworm seed or rearing unit, a data and survey consulting service, and a recorded biology course all give you pricing control and repeat income that a fixed salary does not. Each needs a licence, capital or an audience, so treat ownership as a later stage after you have worked on a live unit.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="field-pay">Field pay and the cost of getting in</h2>

  <p>
    Pay lines mean little without the fee and the contract length beside them. This ledger uses notices and official pages, and it leaves out the unsourced ranges that most career blogs print.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--money">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Route</th>
          <th>Cost or entry rule</th>
          <th>Pay signal</th>
          <th>The sum</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {payRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Route"><strong>{row.route}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Cost or entry rule">{row.cost}</td>
            <td data-label="Pay signal">{row.pay}</td>
            <td data-label="The sum">{row.sum}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Pay figures here are indicative ranges and averages from public notices, not exact or guaranteed amounts. Pay differs by city, employer, skill and year. Check current job listings and ask people working in the role before you decide. Contract notices are for the dates shown and can change.
  </p>

  <p>
    Now the money rule. As a strict planning heuristic, not a law, keep college cost near 10 percent of your total education budget and treat 20 percent as a caution line that needs course-specific proof. On a Rs 10 lakh budget that is Rs 1 lakh and Rs 2 lakh. A Rs 6 lakh programme is 60 percent of it.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Wildlife fieldwork is a calling that pays like a stipend for many years. The route is worth it for people who would do the work anyway, who have a second skill that pays, and who have family support for contracts that renew one year at a time. If you need a steady rising income by your late twenties, put data, fisheries or a business layer first and keep wildlife as the place you volunteer and publish.
    </p>
    <p>
      A degree is the licence for a lab or a notice. The skills around it, field method, GIS, statistics and clear writing, can be learned from good YouTube channels, official manuals and low-cost courses. Check the instructor and the last update, and pay only for feedback or a credential an employer asks for. A folder of certificates is not proof. A finished survey is.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    The other Rs 8 to 9 lakh of a Rs 10 lakh budget is what pays for a laptop, GIS and statistics learning, travel to field sites, living costs during unpaid or low-paid months, and the second skill. If animals plus a clinic-style income is your goal, veterinary medicine has a clearer licence and practice route, but it is a separate degree with its own entrance and seats. For a stronger ceiling from the same interest, the data route above and the fisheries degree route are the better-placed alternatives.
  </p>

  <h2 id="funnels">The second filter in wildlife and research</h2>

  <p>
    Wildlife and research each have two contests: a hard entry stage and a separate hard advancement stage. Clearing the first does not clear the second.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Ladder</th>
          <th>First filter</th>
          <th>Second filter</th>
          <th>The sum</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {funnelRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Ladder"><strong>{row.ladder}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="First filter">{row.first}</td>
            <td data-label="Second filter">{row.second}</td>
            <td data-label="The sum">{row.note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">What this means for you</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>Treat a government forest post as a bonus on top of a plan that pays without it. Exam preparation uses months of income-free time.</li>
      <li>Keep one skill running that survives a failed attempt: data, teaching, or a small farm-service side income.</li>
      <li>Ask every programme how many past students got a paid role, and at what monthly pay.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2 id="who-pays">Who pays, and how they trust you</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>The skill portfolio decides the route, not the degree name:</strong> one core skill (field method, lab method or farm practice), one multiplier (GIS, statistics, English and a local language, simple selling) and proof someone can check.
  </p>

  <p>
    Every route has a payer and a way that payer decides to trust someone. Most zoology students are never taught this. Keep it basic: know who pays, show proof they can check, follow up, and ask for referrals. This promises no income. It makes the first conversation easier.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Route</th>
          <th>Who pays</th>
          <th>How they find and trust you</th>
          <th>First sales or marketing skill</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {whoPaysRows.map((row) => (
