            <td data-label="First sales or marketing skill">{row.skill}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="week-grid">
    {weekCards.map((item) => (
      <div class="week-card">
        <div class="week-card__who">{item.who}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="dissection">What changed in zoology practicals</h2>

  <p>
    UGC guidelines posted in November 2011 told zoology departments to phase out dissection: BSc students should not use animals in experiments, teachers may dissect one species for demonstration, and MSc students may dissect two. Field visits and digital tools were to take its place. The result is that a student can finish a degree with little hands-on animal work. Employers who run labs and field projects notice.
  </p>
  <p>
    Close that gap yourself. Keep a dated field notebook, join a local bird or butterfly count, volunteer with a rescue group or a pond-farming family, and learn the same methods the projects use.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">What AI changes in zoology work</h2>

  <p>
    Species recognition from camera-trap images is the clearest case. Tools such as Wildlife Insights label images that used to take researchers days, and TrailGuard AI cameras sent tiger alerts to rangers in a two-month, 12-camera trial in the Kanha-Pench corridor, according to the developers. That shrinks junior image-sorting and data-entry work.
  </p>
  <p>
    It does not shrink fieldwork, animal handling, farmer trust or the judgment about what a pattern means. Start now: learn to check AI labels against your own, and keep a log of what the tool got wrong. Later, as tools and adoption mature: build a small workflow, such as a cleaning template for survey data, that saves a project team time and that you can show. No multiplier is guaranteed.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checkpoint">The 4-Checkpoint Protocol</h2>

  <p>
    The 4-Checkpoint Protocol turns a feeling into a comparison. Run each of your two shortlisted routes through all four checks.
  </p>

